 both what did we get with this  you know we got unneeded application  logic we need to handle both cases there  is opposed to eat and if there isn't  more code in general means more  maintenance so anytime you change some  commenting logic you need to reflect  that in both scenarios  what about like if you try to introduce  caching are you how i'm going to have  two different strategies for these and  then  more code means more tests you gotta  test both cases out right  so i would say on inputs you know or you  know  data that we receive from the client  it's easy to go from required to  optional but it's not easy to go the  other way around  strict to lose gives you more control it  gives the client less flexibility but  it's not the flexibility that they've  ever asked for that gives you more  flexibility in terms of changing  but lose the strict if you go from  optional to required is a breaking  change because you know certain amount  of clients might actually stop working  the ones that  might have not been sending that  uh value at all  okay and then the second part of the  second principle that i want to talk  about is breaks first or defensive  programming i want to talk about how you  should ensure that your api is not going  to get abused let's go with the same  example  let's imagine we have a requirement to  ability to fetch comments for a post  now the friendly api developer might say  you know let's just give them an array  of comments we don't need pagination or  a better version of this we don't need  pagination yet  now let's imagine we have this same kind  of comments query or endpoint and it  returns just an array of comments  the friendly api developer might you  know justify this with we won't have  more than five comments per post it's  all good  now you know six months later  they're supposed with hundreds of  comments so all of a sudden we do  need some kind of pagination otherwise  you know we  have kind of like a bad performance  experience  now the developer might say let's add  pagination well now what happens now we  let's say we  throw some kind of paginated comments  back really doesn't matter what it looks  like we're introducing a breaking change  and we're introducing a new query uh or  a new endpoint you know either one of  those either breaking change or a new  query or endpoint so nothing is really  that pleasurable about it  and you know the family api developer  might say work is done let's go home  that's it  now  you know what happens if a client sends  out a comment like this you know the  client might have been lazy to actually  do paginated requests so they might send  you know just give me the first thousand  records you know what happened well the  friendly api developer didn't add limits  imagination  i'd say generally adding pagination  limits of breaks of sorts uh from the  beginning makes a lot more sense so you  know you might  end up saying something like you know  requesting a thousand records exceeds  the first limit of 100 records so it's  super hard to add it retroactively but  super easy to adjust in the future you  can always put the number  to go up you can  let it go down  okay so  how to avoid these overheads  now i wanna  i would say adopt scheme of frizz design  you know figure out what is your domain  and for whom you're building your api  don't play guessing games don't couple  your apis to specific implementations  maybe today you have a relational  database that is backing some of this  data tomorrow it might be an api that  won't have the same fields and you might  bring yourself more trouble than  anything  and then talk to your clients involve  them early in the process figure out  what their needs are and then try to be  speculative  and then be responsive to your clients  requests as you're building out a  minimal schema that is not overloaded  you have to be responsive to feature  requests  there's always exceptions  to these rules you know some exceptions  that my you might see is like if you're  building a platform api there might be a  little bit of speculation here if you're  building a platform and trying to get  ahead of competitors to try to drive  user adoption but even then i would kind  of question how much this kind of  speculation is worth it  or if you have constraints due to a  release cycle and this has been an  interesting story that was told to me by  a co-worker that worked on apis that  were deployed on premises for their  clients so they would release these once  every six months and they would have to  do thousands of deployments every time  they release so if they miss the field  that a client might need they would have  to repeat the same redeployments which  would be very costly this is very unique  and probably most of us are not in this  situation  now in conclusion what i want to say  is that redundant work slows down  progress on important features you might  be able to spend your time more wisely  by improving your api documentation  improving slow queries etc exposing too  much data can be highly unproductive you  know there is a saying the road to hell  is paid with good intentions even though  we might think we're doing something  good for our api clients and being a  good partner  speculatively building apis  is well intended but with a negative  result  so  thank you all  this is my twitter handle if you want to  talk more about this topic let's talk  afterwards or reach out to me on twitter  and as i said i work as a senior  software engineer at netflix we have a  bunch of openings if you're ever  interested in building apis at netflix  reach out to me and let's talk
 

